= Lessons learned =

V1 produced valuable architectural ideas and enough populated content to expose where the model failed in practice.

== What worked ==
* MediaWiki as a durable public knowledge substrate.
* Modular/transcludable content as a goal.
* Distinguishing concepts/theories from implementations/projects.
* Knowledge domains and cross-domain relationships.
* Explicit evidence, provenance, and epistemic qualification.
* Portals, graphs, and generated navigation as useful interfaces.
* Human-and-machine readability as a design constraint.

== What failed ==
* Treating MediaWiki like an external drive or directory tree.
* Using Main namespace for governance, schemas, components, indexes, audits, and ordinary knowledge simultaneously.
* Creating pseudo-namespaces and title conventions instead of using configured namespaces.
* Mixing Domain, Scope, Book Matter, entity class, epistemics, and navigation into categories.
* Writing extensive documentation about schemas while barely instantiating the schemas.
* Using duplicate titles and Revised variants instead of revision history and redirects.
* Creating component pages and section fragments in Main without a stable transclusion model.
* Accumulating extensions without clearly separating core, experimental, and specialized capability roles.

== V2 response ==
V2 keeps the original ambition but reduces the primitives. Native MediaWiki structure comes first; semantic metadata remains small; Book Matter represents content anatomy; provenance is explicit; navigation is generated from the model.

